<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/065B">Example <span data-tag="065B">9.3.2.15</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a set. For every regular cardinal $\kappa $, let $\operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \kappa }(S)$ denote the nerve of the partially ordered set of $\kappa $-small subsets of $S$. For every regular cardinal $\lambda \geq \kappa $, Proposition <a data-tag="0651" href="/tag/0651">9.3.2.1</a> and Variant <a data-tag="064L" href="/tag/064L">9.2.5.11</a> supplies a fully faithful functor </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }( \operatorname{Sub}_{&lt; \kappa }(S) ) \hookrightarrow \operatorname{Sub}_{ &lt; \lambda }(S). \]
</div>
<p> This functor is an equivalence if and only if every $\lambda $-small subset $S' \subseteq S$ can be realized as a $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered union of $\kappa $-small subsets of $S'$ (Remark <a data-tag="0654" href="/tag/0654">9.3.2.4</a>). Assuming that $S$ has cardinality $\geq \lambda $, this is equivalent to the requirement that $\kappa \trianglelefteq \lambda $ (see Definition <a data-tag="061K" href="/tag/061K">9.1.7.5</a>). </p>
</article>
